<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 4 – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors of Things that Go</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Red Bike.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Fill in a form with their name and age with adult help. Interactive: Write very simple sentences with assistance, even if errors are present.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can complete a simple form with teacher assistance, filling in details about transportation items (e.g., writing "red" for the color of a bus). Can write simple words independently to label transportation items on drawings (e.g., "bus," "car"). Can write very simple sentences with assistance, even if errors are present (e.g., "This is a green car.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays large flashcards of different colored vehicles (e.g., a blue bus, a green car, a yellow taxi, a red bike). The teacher says the color and vehicle, e.g., "A blue bus!" Students repeat chorally. The teacher then asks, "What color is the bus?" and students respond, "Blue!" The teacher emphasizes the initial sounds of words like /k/ in "car" and /b/ in "bus" as students repeat.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a red bike. The teacher slowly writes the words "red bike" on the board, pointing to each word as it is written and saying, "This is a red bike." The teacher then points to the flashcard and to the written words. The teacher demonstrates how to trace letters by tracing "red" and "bike" in the air, then on a worksheet, connecting the written word to the picture.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the written word "red") "Is this 'blue'?" (Expected answer: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the written word "bike") "Is this a 'car'?" (Expected answer: "No, bike!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors of Things that Go")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large chart or projector slide displaying simple, clear sentences and matching illustrations of colored transportation items (e.g., "This is a red bike.", "It is a green car.", "That is a blue bus."). The text is large and easy to read for 1st graders. Genre: Descriptive sentences. Register: Simple and direct. Format: Illustrated sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing objects: When describing colors in transportation, use phrases that describe vehicles, like "It is a red bike." "That is a green car." or "It's a blue bus."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads each sentence aloud, pointing to the words as they are read. Students repeat chorally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> The teacher says, "Point to 'That is a blue bus.'" Students point to the corresponding sentence and picture on the chart.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identify the Color/Vehicle:</b> The teacher asks, "What color is this (pointing to a yellow taxi picture)?" Students respond "Yellow!" The teacher asks, "What is this (pointing to a truck picture)?" Students respond "Truck!" The teacher models politeness: "Please, point to the blue car."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing simple sentences using "Color + noun" structure (e.g., "green car," "red bike"). Vocabulary: car, bus, bike, truck, train, taxi, green, blue, red, yellow, purple, orange. Grammar: Present simple "It is a..." or "This is a...".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Tracing and Filling:</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of vehicles. Below each picture, there is a simple sentence with missing words (e.g., "This is a ____ ____." with a picture of a green car, or "__ed b__ke"). Students trace the words provided (e.g., "This is a") and then fill in the missing color and vehicle words from a small word bank (e.g., "red," "bike," "green," "car") or by copying from the board with teacher assistance. The teacher circulates, offering help and reinforcing phonemic awareness by asking, "What sound does 'car' start with?" (/k/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Guided Sentence Construction:</b> The teacher shows a flashcard (e.g., a blue train). Students use a sentence frame provided on their worksheet (e.g., "It is a ______ ______.") to write a complete sentence about the picture. The teacher guides them by first asking, "What color is the train?" and then "What is it?" and then prompts them to write the words into the blanks.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Point and Write:</b> In pairs, students show each other one of their completed sentences or a picture they drew and labeled. Student A says, "It's a yellow taxi." Student B points to the corresponding picture/sentence on Student A's paper. Then Student B attempts to write a new sentence about a different vehicle from the worksheet, with Student A offering non-verbal encouragement (e.g., pointing to the word bank if needed). The teacher provides heavy scaffolding, ensuring students are primarily focused on the writing task, even if words are misspelled.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors of Things that Go")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual "My Favorite Ride" form completion and sentence writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students receive a simple form titled "My Favorite Ride". The form includes spaces for: "My Name: _______" and "My Age: _______" (with adult help). Below that, there are three blank boxes. In each box, students are instructed to draw a transportation item (car, bus, bike, etc.) and color it. Below each drawing box, there are two lines for writing: "Color: _______" and "Vehicle: _______". Finally, there is a line for writing a simple sentence, such as "This is a _______ ______." for each drawing. The teacher provides words on the board for reference (e.g., red, blue, green, car, bus, bike). Students complete the form, focusing on writing the color and vehicle names correctly, and attempting the simple descriptive sentence with teacher assistance. For example, a student draws a green bus, then writes "Color: green", "Vehicle: bus", and "This is a green bus." This activity directly targets "writing simple words independently" and "writing very simple sentences with assistance" while "filling in a form."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Favorite Ride" forms. Before submission, students briefly share their completed form with a peer. The peer points to the color and vehicle they wrote, saying the words aloud. This encourages basic peer checking and reinforces vocabulary. The teacher collects the forms for individual assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Completion of "My Name" and "My Age" fields (with assistance).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility of written color and vehicle words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of target vocabulary (at least 2 color words, 2 vehicle words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempt to write at least one simple sentence ("This is a...").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a small paper or raise fingers to answer:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I write the name of a color? (Thumbs up / Thumbs down)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I write the name of a vehicle? (Thumbs up / Thumbs down)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new word did you write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or hard about writing the sentences?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to correctly fill in the color and vehicle names with assistance. The simple sentence writing (e.g., "This is a red bike.") was challenging for some, requiring significant tracing or copying. What adjustments are needed for next lesson? More explicit modeling of letter formation and simple sentence structure will be beneficial. Scaffolding for writing needs to continue with word banks and sentence frames. For the next lesson, consider using visual cues and sentence starters to help students summarize what they have learned.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This writing lesson has prepared students to summarize and integrate their knowledge of vehicles and colors. In Lesson 5, students will use their learned vocabulary and writing skills to complete a project that involves describing their favorite transportation, consolidating their learning in a mediated activity.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>